>> Armour Class; in the late game almost all monsters' physical attacks are
>> practically irrelevant.
>
>Maybe I am generally underprepared. I usually don't have 200+ HPs, rather
>100 or less when I face my first minotaur. Generally it very much depends
>on the actual game whether I can get a "high enough" AC. It also depends
>on whether I play a class with an physical damage reducing artifact. Last
>but not least I had games where I did not find any scroll of enchant armor
>until the late game (or had to wish for one).

Assuming all items to be undamaged, unenchanted items of the "magical"
flavours:

Donations: 9 points
DSM: 9 points
Headgear: 1 point
Gloves: 1 point
Boots: 1 point
Cloak: 1 point

That's AC -12 right there, without using a single scroll of enchant
armor. That subtracts 1d12 from the damage of each individual melee
attack that comes your way. This, for instance, *halves* the expected
damage output of a Jabberwock.

>> Monsters; monsters of the same nominal difficulty rating vary wildly in
>> difficulty.
>
>I don't know what you mean here.

Monsters have an algorithmically determined "difficulty" rating which is
used by the game to control monster generation. This difficulty rating
often bears no meaningful relation to how dangerous the monster is under
conditions where it can be generated.

>> Some monsters are almost always speedbumps when you find them;
>> some are always dangerous.
>
>I would classify that as diversity. (Another critique of Nethack; lacking
>diversity.) To have diversity and balance is a hard task that Nethack has
>achieved quite well. YMMV.

A monster that has just come in-depth should always be at least *some*
kind of threat to the player - even if it's just that it takes long
enough to kill to be inconvenient when other monsters are around and
does enough damage that you can't just ignore it completely.

In the late game, a decently prepared player finds that all monsters are
Rodney, Demogorgon, Pestilence, or a greasy smear of guts on the
windscreen - and Demogorgon can be made into such a smear simply by
engraving the name of the Lady of Stars on the dungeon floor.

>> #twoweapon; there's a wide variety of combat options, one of which is
>> always superior if you can use it.
>
>But that's class depending; classes that have some advantageous properties
>lack some other properties (and v.v.). That's a positive diversity/balance
>effect as far as I judge.

Let me emphasise:

If you *can* twoweapon effectively (which is to say, you can twoweapon
*at all*; monster ACs are with a few exceptions so pitifully easy to
overcome that even level 17 or so characters never miss anything except
an exceptionally fortunate armor-wearing monster), it is (barring a lack
of resources which Nethack's game mechanics make unlikely even if you
*don't* pudding farm) guaranteed to be more effective than the use of
either a two-handed weapon *or* a one-handed weapon and a shield.

And that is without considering the hazard drawback of wielding
two-handed weapons.

Overall, I cannot imagine Nethack appearing balanced to an experienced
player unless they have some manner of mental blind spot that makes the
tedious-but-easy exploits invisible to them.
